{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792698909,"nonce":"fjoC/G9vd+YwdlTakPsZyscEgqU=","title":"TypeScript: module-level variables visible inside functions","message":"Functions are lowered before the top-level body, so a top-level let, var or non-literal const is not visible inside a function: the use reports \"cannot find name\" (a type error, wrongly, since the program is valid). Top-level const declarations that fold to a literal are now substituted at each use; everything else is still missing: mutable top-level variables, constants initialized from calls or aggregates (arrays, class instances), and reads that must observe a later write.\n\nNeeded: lower these as module globals (TypedDeclaration::Variable, as the Python frontend does for its globals) with correct initialization order and ownership for heap values; report an explicit Unsupported until then instead of a type error. Substituted constants also skip the temporal dead zone: a function called before the const line runs sees the value instead of a ReferenceError.\n\nBenchmark code commonly keeps data and counters at module level, so this blocks real kernels beyond the literal-constant case.","files":null}]}